            <ArticleTitle>Unrecognized stenosis by angiography documented by intravascular ultrasound imaging.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>198-201</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This report documents how intravascular ultrasound imaging was used to diagnose a short &quot;napkin-ring&quot; stenosis that was missed by coronary angiography. Intravascular ultrasound revealed a lumen of 2.6 x 2.5 mm in diameter and 5.0 mm2 in cross-sectional area, with a residual atheroma that occluded 63% of available cross-sectional area at the stenosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
